WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.984
Open starter dot py and find wins.

00:00:02.984 --> 00:00:07.042
In a scratch copy, I'll replace the
exception with my first guess.

00:00:07.042 --> 00:00:10.158
Give the players a point whenever their
answers match.

00:00:10.158 --> 00:00:12.344
Which pair of settings breaks it?

00:00:14.875 --> 00:00:16.325
Run the Game check.

00:00:16.325 --> 00:00:17.864
Four cases fail.

00:00:17.864 --> 00:00:21.531
Here, both stations got a one, both
answered zero,

00:00:21.531 --> 00:00:23.720
and our scorer gave them a point.

00:00:23.720 --> 00:00:25.645
The check expected a loss.

00:00:26.250 --> 00:00:28.008
Let's fix that case.

00:00:28.008 --> 00:00:31.216
If both settings are one, the answers
have to differ.

00:00:31.216 --> 00:00:33.974
For every other setting, they have to
match.

00:00:34.625 --> 00:00:36.488
Run the same check again.

00:00:36.488 --> 00:00:37.534
It passes.

00:00:37.534 --> 00:00:41.919
The one test tries all four settings
against all four possible pairs

00:00:41.919 --> 00:00:42.980
of answers.

00:00:43.500 --> 00:00:48.303
Now use the finished reporter, lab dot
py, to inspect a different run.

00:00:48.303 --> 00:00:50.713
These boxes always answered zero.

00:00:50.713 --> 00:00:54.084
Pause and count how many trials could
possibly lose.

00:00:56.625 --> 00:00:57.639
Only three.

00:00:57.639 --> 00:01:02.621
The boxes won ninety-seven times because
the one-one setting hardly appeared.

00:01:02.621 --> 00:01:05.862
Each setting has the same correlation,
plus one.

00:01:05.862 --> 00:01:10.149
Add the first three, subtract the last,
and the Bell score is two,

00:01:10.149 --> 00:01:12.148
right at the classical bound.
